a  pattern here and hopefully we'll be able  to get it out  there and do something with the data  okay so I'll give an overview of the  pipeline in words first and then code  later so first step of the pipeline is  gonna be we're gonna pull in a bunch of  tweets either in a first big batch or  any new ones as they come up  then we're gonna extract the addresses  out of those tweets send the addresses  to the Google Maps API to get the  latitudes and longitudes of those  addresses and then some downstream stuff  too okay so this is all in this one  repository so all the functions are  defined in this didn't start at our file  which will source in now okay so in code  this is what the pipeline looks like  we'll have a get tweets function which  is going to in this case get 3,000  tweets and then save them all to a file  then we'll pull the addresses out of  those fires out of those tweets then  send the addresses to Google Maps to get  the latitude longitude and then count up  the number of fires per latitude and  longitude combination and try and plot  them on a map so you might be sort of  noticing that this is like a pretty  linear kind of pipeline we're going from  tweets to addresses to lat/long and then  counting stuff up and that's kind of why  it's a good candidate for may be using  Drake in this case so you might be  wondering like what is Drake Drake is a  workflow manager for your R code so it  makes basically it makes changing your  code easier makes you have less to worry  about when you change your code so the  main idea is that Drake will only  rebuild the parts of your analysis that  you need to rebuild when things get out  of date and their tagline is so they  what get done what gets done stays done  so it's built and maintained by will  landau if you have any complaints about  the package or any feature requests he's  very responsive ok so I kind of came to  Drake because probably like a lot of you  my analysis directories often looks kind  of like this which is always seem like a  little bit happy to me doesn't scale  very well if you want to put another  file in here like between four and five  you got to reorder everything  you want to like reorganize your  sub-directories it can be kind of a pain  so your pipeline depends on your file  naming being up to date sourcing things  in the right order knowing when the  input data changes no one kind of which  functions and objects depend on which  other objects and functions and then  when something inevitably breaks how do  you know where it broke and can you be  sure that the intermediate data stores  are up-to-date or is it the safest thing  to just rerun the entire pipeline  soup-to-nuts again usually but like  that's kind of a pain so Drake is a nice  solution to this because it creates a  tidy data frame that shows how the  pieces of your pipeline fit together it  also creates a dependency graph of all  inputs and outputs in your pipeline  which is really good for reproducibility  especially when you use it in  conjunction with them version control  tons of support for parallel and  distributed computing backends and it's  all written in R so no writing make  files or anything like that ok  so when I'm talking about a dependency  graph it kind of looks like this and  Drake lets you visualize stuff like this  so circle means like some sort of  imported thing like the empty cars data  set triangle as a function so he's  written some function called random rows  and then those all feed into the  simulate function which generates small  and so you can kind of see how  everything fits together and what's  feeding into what green means up to date  this target has been just made so it's  up to date and none of the code has  changed if it were not up to date it  would be black ok so three pieces of  vocab targets are the objects like that  green dot that Drake generates commands  are the pieces of our code that produced  those targets and then plans wrap up the  relationship between targets and  commands ok so you can think of like a  plan as kind of that top level script  where you're like sourcing everything in  and then generating the final output the  difference is that a plan knows about  the dependencies in your code so it's a  little bit of a better solution  okay so when you're thinking about Drake  normally how I go about this is I store  all my functions in one file or one or  two files and then you create a plan  using this Drake plan function here so  if you're looking at this targets are on  the left over here and then commands are  on the right  so we're generating this target clean  data from the command clean my raw data  so clean my would be a function and then  raw data would be some data object and  then you just run make a plan and so  what Drake is gonna do is it's going to  take this plan object that we've created  and that's a data frame so it knows the  relationship the one-to-one relationship  between every target and the command  that's used to generate it and then when  you run make plan the first time it's  going to run the entire thing from  scratch and then every subsequent time  that you run make plan it's only going  to rebuild the targets that are out of  date and everything that's downstream of  those targets okay so what makes a  target out of date you can set trigger  so you can say like if this is true then  always rebuild this particular target or  something used to generate that target  or one of its upstream targets has  changed so that could be either code or  data has changed so if we're looking at  just like this highlighted row here  generating the target results from the  command analyzed my clean data Drake  will know that results depends on both  the object clean data and the function  analyze mine because they're part of the  command and so if either of those  changes if you change any code in  analyze my Drake will know that and  it'll know that results is out of date  it'll als